@{
    ViewBag.Title = "Performance Rules";
}

@section css
{
    @Styles.Render("~/bundles/controls/css/aspmvc")
}

<div class="mdi-action-info help" title="More information">
    <ul class="info">
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span> Guidelines that you should never skip and should be applicable to all situations.</li>
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">**</span> Strongly recommended guidelines.</li>
        <li><span class="badge lbl-importance">*</span> Recommended guidelines that may not be applicable in all situations.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
<div class="col-sm-12" id="doc-content">
    <ul class="rules unordered">
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title first"><span class="rule-item">OW5021 -</span> CONSIDER partial page updates using AJAX for reducing bandwidth <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                One way to mitigate performance issues involving both server processing and request size is to use
                asynchronous JavaScript (AJAX) to do partial page updates. ASP.NET MVC has built-in AJAX support, which
                help the facilities. The performance benefit occurs because the pattern reduces the amount of processing
                that the server must do to render a request and reduce the size of the HTML fragment.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW5022 -</span> DO NOT overuses session, instead use TempData for short live (intra-request) storage <span class="badge lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                The problem with placing these objects in the Session is that can bog down the server by having to store
                extra information that may only be necessary across a redirect. The correct way to store these temporary
                variables across a redirect is by using the TempData dictionary.
            </p>
            <p>For example:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    [HttpPost]
                    public ActionResult LogIn(Seller person)
                    {
                        TempData["name"] = person.Name;
                        return RedirectToAction("ItemUpload");
                    }
                    public ActionResult ItemUpload()
                    {
                        string name = TempData["name"].ToString();
                        ViewBag.Name = name;
                        return View();
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW5023 -</span> USE an OutputCache Filter for static pages <span class="badge lbl-importance">**</span></h2>
            <p>
                Use OutputCache attribute when you are returning less frequently updated data; your home page
                would be a good candidate. This technique can be used for both HTML and JSON data types. You should
                only specify the cache profile name while using it; do not specify anything else. You can use the output
                cache session of the Web.config file if you need to fine tune caching.
            </p>
            <p>For example:</p>
            <div class="example-code">
                <pre class="brush: csharp">
                    [AcceptVerbs(HttpVerbs.Get), OutputCache(CacheProfile = "Dashboard")]
                    public ActionResult Dashboard(string userName, StoryListTab tab, OrderBy orderBy, int? page)
                    {
                    }
                </pre>
            </div>
        </li>
        <li>
            <h2 class="sub-title"><span class="rule-item">OW5024 -</span> CONSIDER using asynchronous controllers for long running requests <span class="badge lbl-importance">***</span></h2>
            <p>
                ASP.NET's threading pool has a default limit of 12 concurrent worker threads per CPU. When the requests
                overload the server's ability to process them, a queue is built up of requests.
            </p>
            <p>When the queue gets too large (5000 requests pending), the server starts responding with 503 (server too busy) errors.</p>
            <p>
                In ASP.NET 4, the number of concurrent threads is set by default to 5000. It is possible to increase the
                default limits, there is a better way to mitigate long running requests by using asynchronous.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</div>
<div class="scroll-top">
    <span class="mdi-editor-publish icon-scroll-top"></span>
</div>
@section scripts
{
    @Scripts.Render("~/bundles/controls/js/aspmvc")
}

